 "blog": {
  "heading": "Notes from the *gallery.*",
  "intro": "Short reads on directing, culture and mentoring new talent.",
  "items": [
   {
    "id": "vlog",
    "category": "Culture",
    "title": "How vlogging changed the way Kenya tells stories",
    "summary": "A look at the evolution of vlogging and what it means for broadcasters.",
    "author": "Ngureh Ryan",
    "date": "",
    "lede": "For a long time, a story only reached an audience after it passed through a studio. Vlogging removed that gate, and broadcasters are still working out what that means.",
    "sections": [
     {
      "kind": "text",
      "heading": "The studio used to be the gatekeeper",
      "body": "Not long ago, telling a story to a wide audience meant access to a transmitter, a camera crew and an editor. The people who owned that equipment decided which stories were told and how.\n\nVlogging changed the starting point. A phone, a ring light and a quiet corner are enough to publish. Suddenly the person who lives the story can also be the one who tells it.",
      "bullets": ""
     },
     {
      "kind": "text",
      "heading": "What creators do differently",
      "body": "Vloggers talk to one person, not to \"the audience\". They speak in the same voice they use with friends, they show the messy parts, and they answer comments within hours. That closeness is the main reason people trust them.\n\nThey also move quickly. A topic can be filmed, edited and posted in a day, while a traditional show plans weeks ahead.",
      "bullets": ""
     },
     {
      "kind": "text",
      "heading": "What broadcasters can learn",
      "body": "Television does not need to copy creators, but it can borrow three habits:",
      "bullets": "Speak plainly and warmly, even on a formal set.\nInvite the audience in: read their comments and questions on air.\nMake room for ordinary voices, not only official ones."
     },
     {
      "kind": "text",
      "heading": "Where television still wins",
      "body": "A live studio brings things a single creator rarely has: a crew that checks facts, a director who keeps the show on time, and a schedule people can rely on. Those strengths matter most when audiences need accurate information fast.",
      "bullets": ""
     },
     {
      "kind": "quote",
      "quote": "The best media of the next decade will keep the discipline of the studio and the closeness of the creator."
     },
     {
      "kind": "text",
      "heading": "Looking ahead",
      "body": "Television, radio and digital are no longer separate worlds. A single idea can start as a segment, continue as a short video and live on as a podcast or post. Storytellers who plan for every platform from the start will reach the most people.",
      "bullets": ""
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "time",
    "category": "Directing",
    "title": "Keeping a live show on time when everything moves",
    "summary": "Lessons on timelines, crews and calm under live pressure.",
    "author": "Ngureh Ryan",
    "date": "",
    "lede": "On a live show, the clock does not care that a guest ran long or a camera failed. A director's job is to keep the show on air, and on time, anyway.",
    "sections": [
     {
      "kind": "text",
      "heading": "The rundown is a plan, not a promise",
      "body": "Every live show starts with a rundown listing each segment and its length. The moment the red light goes on, it begins to drift. A guest answers for longer, a clip loads late, a story breaks.\n\nThe skill is not sticking rigidly to the page. It is knowing what can change without the viewer noticing.",
      "bullets": ""
     },
     {
      "kind": "text",
      "heading": "Build in elastic segments",
      "body": "Good rundowns include pieces that can stretch or shrink. A prepared extra question, a short backup clip or an interview that can be cut to two minutes gives the director room to move.",
      "bullets": "Mark which segments can grow and which are fixed.\nKeep one short filler ready at all times.\nKnow your hard stop, such as the next programme or a live cross."
     },
     {
      "kind": "text",
      "heading": "Cue early, speak calmly",
      "body": "Cues given early let presenters, camera operators and the gallery prepare. Calling a shot at the last second creates panic; calling it a beat sooner creates smoothness.\n\nTone matters as much as timing. A calm, clear voice on talkback spreads through the whole crew. A tense one does the same.",
      "bullets": ""
     },
     {
      "kind": "quote",
      "quote": "A calm gallery is a fast gallery."
     },
     {
      "kind": "text",
      "heading": "Talk to the floor",
      "body": "The floor manager is the director's link to the presenters. Simple hand signals such as \"stretch\", \"wrap\" and \"30 seconds\" let time changes reach the set without a single word on air.",
      "bullets": ""
     },
     {
      "kind": "text",
      "heading": "When something goes wrong",
      "body": "Equipment fails and guests cancel. What matters is how quickly a decision is made. Name the problem, pick the backup, tell the crew, and move on. Debating on talkback costs seconds that the show does not have.",
      "bullets": ""
     },
     {
      "kind": "text",
      "heading": "Review after every show",
      "body": "A short debrief with the crew, covering what ran long, what worked and what to change next time, turns each broadcast into practice for the next one.",
      "bullets": ""
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "mentor",
    "category": "Mentorship",
    "title": "Training the next generation of media practitioners",
    "summary": "What new producers and hosts need most from their first year.",
    "author": "Ngureh Ryan",
    "date": "",
    "lede": "Most people who join a studio team learn the job in the first twelve months. What they receive in that time shapes the kind of practitioners they become.",
    "sections": [
     {
      "kind": "text",
      "heading": "Start with clear roles",
      "body": "New producers, hosts and crew members often feel lost simply because nobody has told them what is expected. A clear description of their role, who they report to and what a good day looks like removes a lot of stress.",
      "bullets": ""
     },
     {
      "kind": "text",
      "heading": "Give safe chances to practise",
      "body": "Studio work cannot be learned from a book alone. Newcomers need time on real equipment and in real rehearsals, where mistakes are allowed and corrected. Mock shows, camera practice and off-air runs build confidence before the red light goes on.",
      "bullets": ""
     },
     {
      "kind": "text",
      "heading": "Pair people with experienced crew",
      "body": "Shadowing a seasoned floor manager, camera operator or producer teaches things that never appear in manuals: how to read a room, when to speak up and how to stay calm. A simple mentor pairing, even for a few hours a week, makes a big difference.",
      "bullets": ""
     },
     {
      "kind": "quote",
      "quote": "Responsibility, given early and with support, builds confidence faster than any lecture."
     },
     {
      "kind": "text",
      "heading": "Give honest, specific feedback",
      "body": "\"Good job\" and \"needs work\" teach very little. Useful feedback names the moment, explains the effect and suggests a change, for example: \"Your intro was warm, but you looked away from the camera on the second line.\"",
      "bullets": "Give feedback soon after the show, while it is fresh.\nPraise specific things as well as pointing out problems.\nAsk the learner what they would do differently."
     },
     {
      "kind": "text",
      "heading": "Let them lead early",
      "body": "Handing a newcomer a small real task, such as a short segment, a social clip or a rundown, shows trust. With a mentor nearby, they learn how to make decisions rather than wait for instructions.",
      "bullets": ""
     },
     {
      "kind": "text",
      "heading": "Keep the door open",
      "body": "The most valuable thing a mentor offers is availability. Practitioners who know they can ask a question without being judged tend to grow faster and stay in the industry longer.",
      "bullets": ""
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
